1
00:00:00,466 --> 00:00:06,566
In February 2023, a message went viral: LV was raising prices by up to 20%.

2
00:00:06,933 --> 00:00:12,266
Before it was even official, long lines had already formed outside stores in Beijing, Shanghai, and Hangzhou.

3
00:00:12,433 --> 00:00:16,700
Some people said they waited an hour just to get in, and the hot items sold out.

4
00:00:16,833 --> 00:00:22,166
Your first reaction to this story is probably: rich people are so strange, the pricier it gets, the more they want it.

5
00:00:22,466 --> 00:00:29,900
Think it through one more step and you land on a theory: the law of demand just doesn't apply to luxury goods.

6
00:00:30,133 --> 00:00:31,933
That's what I thought at first too.

7
00:00:31,966 --> 00:00:37,466
But once I looked closely at what that line was actually fighting for, it turned out to be the exact opposite.

8
00:00:37,633 --> 00:00:42,166
This line that keeps growing as the price rises is actually the law of demand working exactly as it should.

9
00:00:42,333 --> 00:00:45,066
This is Pixel Breath's 100 Principles of Economics.

10
00:00:45,066 --> 00:00:47,100
Episode 7: the law of demand.

11
00:00:47,366 --> 00:00:51,700
Last time I ended by asking: once bubble tea gets more expensive, do you still line up for it?

12
00:00:51,933 --> 00:00:57,466
This time we're swapping in a much pricier line to see if it follows the same rule.

13
00:00:57,933 --> 00:01:00,133
First, let's get the law of demand straight.

14
00:01:00,366 --> 00:01:08,266
All else being equal, the higher the price of something, the less people want to buy, and the lower the price, the more they buy.

15
00:01:08,566 --> 00:01:12,133
Notice the catch in that sentence: it's comparing the same thing.

16
00:01:12,533 --> 00:01:16,933
So let's look at what the people lining up in early February were actually comparing.

17
00:01:17,633 --> 00:01:20,000
Say a bag is priced at 20,000 yuan today.

18
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:23,333
After the price hike on the 18th, the same bag becomes 24,000 yuan.

19
00:01:23,566 --> 00:01:31,066
The people at the door aren't thinking about whether the bag is expensive — they're thinking buying today is 4,000 yuan cheaper than buying next week.

20
00:01:31,266 --> 00:01:35,333
To them, today's bag is basically a bag at a 17% discount.

21
00:01:35,566 --> 00:01:38,000
Lower price, more buyers.

22
00:01:38,133 --> 00:01:40,933
See, this doesn't violate the law of demand at all.

23
00:01:41,133 --> 00:01:45,300
It's just that what people are comparing is today's price versus next week's price.

24
00:01:46,233 --> 00:01:48,000
Actually, you and I have both done this.

25
00:01:48,366 --> 00:01:53,533
A sign at the supermarket says prices go up tomorrow — don't you grab an extra bag or two of rice?

26
00:01:54,000 --> 00:02:01,400
The people lining up that day and you grabbing extra rice at the supermarket are the same move, just rice swapped for a handbag.

27
00:02:01,800 --> 00:02:04,300
There's another type of person who thinks even further ahead.

28
00:02:04,533 --> 00:02:15,166
A consumer in Beijing told a reporter that a Chanel bag she bought a few years ago has gone up more than 30,000 yuan at the counter since — to her, that's more stable than buying stocks or funds.

29
00:02:15,333 --> 00:02:19,733
Notice: in her mind, the price of that bag is no longer the number on the counter.

30
00:02:19,800 --> 00:02:24,533
It's what she paid, minus what she could resell it for.

31
00:02:25,033 --> 00:02:32,666
If she believes the bag will sell for even more next year, owning it is basically free for her — maybe even profitable.

32
00:02:32,866 --> 00:02:36,700
When the real price is that low, of course a lot of people want in.

33
00:02:36,800 --> 00:02:45,900
So that line outside the door has two kinds of people: ones racing to lock in the old price before the hike, and ones racing to grab something they think will appreciate.

34
00:02:46,266 --> 00:02:49,166
Neither group is buying because it got more expensive.

35
00:02:49,400 --> 00:02:53,766
Quite the opposite — both of them believe they're getting the cheaper deal.

36
00:02:55,166 --> 00:02:56,433
So here's the question.

37
00:02:56,433 --> 00:03:01,466
If the law of demand is real and prices just keep climbing year after year, when does it finally kick in?

38
00:03:01,866 --> 00:03:08,400
Chanel's most classic bag, the CF, roughly doubled in price between 2019 and 2024.

39
00:03:08,733 --> 00:03:14,200
Store staff told reporters Chanel raises prices twice a year, about 10% each time.

40
00:03:14,333 --> 00:03:17,200
For several years, it seemed like no matter how much it went up, people kept buying.

41
00:03:17,400 --> 00:03:19,900
But in 2024, the bill came due.

42
00:03:20,200 --> 00:03:33,500
Bain & Company and the Italian luxury goods association published a report that November with one striking number: over the past two years, about 50 million luxury customers worldwide had either stopped buying or been priced out of the market.

43
00:03:33,833 --> 00:03:37,933
Those two years line up almost exactly with that February when the lines started forming.

44
00:03:38,066 --> 00:03:44,766
The report gave three reasons: economic uncertainty, brands that kept raising prices, and young people increasingly unwilling to pay up.

45
00:03:45,066 --> 00:03:50,366
50 million people is roughly the population of Spain, walking out of luxury stores.

46
00:03:50,566 --> 00:03:56,966
That year, Chanel's official sales came in at 18.7 billion dollars, down 4.3% from the year before.

47
00:03:57,200 --> 00:04:00,333
Honestly, when I saw those numbers, I had to pause for a second.

48
00:04:00,733 --> 00:04:05,400
Because in 2024, if you walked by Chanel at SKP, there were probably still people waiting outside.

49
00:04:05,600 --> 00:04:06,966
And that's exactly where the problem is.

50
00:04:07,033 --> 00:04:10,133
The 50 million who left weren't the ones at the front of the line.

51
00:04:10,400 --> 00:04:16,400
Bain's report is clear: spending from the very top tier of big spenders actually kept climbing.

52
00:04:16,566 --> 00:04:23,166
The ones who left were more often people who scraped together savings for months to buy an entry-level piece, young people especially.

53
00:04:23,400 --> 00:04:32,533
These people didn't vanish entirely either — many just changed how they bought: a bottle of perfume, a lipstick, an outlet store, or just secondhand.

54
00:04:32,766 --> 00:04:36,000
This is what the law of demand looks like when it plays out across a crowd.

55
00:04:36,366 --> 00:04:41,366
Every time the price ticks up a notch, the first to leave is always the person at the back of the line who could take it or leave it.

56
00:04:41,633 --> 00:04:47,966
The line at the door doesn't look any shorter, because the people at the very front never cared much about price to begin with.

57
00:04:49,866 --> 00:04:57,700
Of course, luxury goods carry something else too: the pricier it is, the more status it signals — some people are buying exactly the fact that others can't afford it.

58
00:04:57,866 --> 00:05:00,800
That's a different effect, and we'll cover it on its own later.

59
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:08,733
For today, just one point: even once you factor in status, the law of demand still isn't broken — it's just that other conditions have changed.

60
00:05:09,000 --> 00:05:13,266
So next time you see something getting pricier and more sought-after, ask yourself two questions.

61
00:05:13,666 --> 00:05:18,666
First: are they chasing the price after the hike, or the price before it?

62
00:05:18,933 --> 00:05:22,066
Most "pricier yet more sought-after" cases are really just chasing that time gap.

63
00:05:22,333 --> 00:05:25,400
Second: are they still buying the same thing?

64
00:05:25,866 --> 00:05:34,966
When everyone believes it'll appreciate, it's an asset; the moment that belief cracks, it turns back into just a bag, and the real price suddenly feels expensive.

65
00:05:35,166 --> 00:05:37,966
These two questions work on you too.

66
00:05:38,166 --> 00:05:42,900
Next time you see "last day at the original price, going up tomorrow," don't rush to buy.

67
00:05:42,933 --> 00:05:47,866
Ask yourself: if this were already the post-hike price, would I still buy it?

68
00:05:48,133 --> 00:05:49,266
If yes, go ahead and buy it.

69
00:05:49,366 --> 00:05:54,100
If no, then what you actually wanted wasn't the thing itself, just that little gap in price.

70
00:05:54,200 --> 00:05:57,700
Back to that hour-long line from February 2023.

71
00:05:57,866 --> 00:05:59,100
It really was long.

72
00:05:59,233 --> 00:06:06,500
But the law of demand never watches how long the line is at the front — it only counts, one by one, who never comes back at the end.

73
00:06:06,666 --> 00:06:13,300
Of the 50 million who left, most were young people and entry-level buyers; the ones who stayed were the wealthiest.

74
00:06:13,766 --> 00:06:19,100
Same price hike — why do some people leave the moment it happens, while others stay no matter how much it rises?

75
00:06:19,400 --> 00:06:21,900
Next time, we'll talk about price elasticity.
